ub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>In the normal state, one of the sister chromatids is stained dark, while the other is stained light. When a reciprocal change occurs, a dark segment and its homologue light counterpart change places leading to alternating dark and light regions throughout the chromatids.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-742-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Comparison of sister chromatid exchange frequencies was performed between the preoperative and postoperative values for etodolac, nimesulid and naproxen sodium.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-742-g002"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Demographic Data and Frequency of Sister Chromatid Exchanges per Metaphases</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-742-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>SCE, sister chromatid exchange; M, male; F, female.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="T2" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>The Differences in the Frequencies of Sister Chromatid Exchange between Preoperative and Postoperative Values of Etodolac, Nimesulid and Naproxen Sodium Were Found to be Statistically Insignificant</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ymj-49-742-i002"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>W, Wilcoxon test; KW, Kruskal Wallis test.</p><p>n : 10 (n : 10 patient).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>